> Basically, some of us were left with the uneasy feeling that these are
> kids in a sandbox. Lots of playing, lots of blue sky plans, but much
> less of a product focus than even Xerox had. Yes, that's possible.
> [...]
> Sure, they _might_ invent the Aptical Fodderer that changes the world,
> but $100 million alone is not the key. Intel, for example, will spend
> more than a _billion_ dollars next year on R&D. No guarantees about
> who changes the world.

I'd like to continue on this because, nowadays, this is a phenomenon
that, IMHO, tends to level the playing field for small entrepreneurs:

An impression I built up in my last years in the Philips Research
organisation is of a change in what meaningful innovation means, and
how difficult it has become to orient and steer private research.

Whether this impression is subjective or not, the development of
wildly new concepts, applications, and hardware is now as important
as the improvement of existing inventions (as highlighted currently
by media merger mania, the rush to the net, the wildly successful
funding of productless startups like 3DO, etc... and as opposed to
the lack of interest in more efficient light bulbs or hohum attitude
to smaller VLSI geometries) Never before has the necessity of being
early in new markets been more emphasized. But the
way you go about making these two different kinds of innovation
happen is completely different. 

On one hand an organisation like Intel can spend hundreds of millions
of dollars on specific problems, and pretty much EXPECT results. A
food giant may spend the same amount of money as Intel does on
semiconductor R&D, and would not get anywhere. On the
other hand, if you decide to try and cash in early on the next media
revolution (or the next distribution channel revolution, etc..), not
only it's very hard to decide what to spend money on, but
it's unlikely that these spendings will do you much more good than
simply watching closely what happens in universities and garage startups
and standing ready to jump on them (or buying content rights for media,
and mail order setups for distribution :-). In this type of situation,
money buys you more people, and money buys you the exclusivity to the
result of their work, but the likelihood of success may not grow
much faster than the proportion of in-house researchers to the total
number of researchers: that's linear growth, but that's not a big
number.

What we are interested in here is the second kind of innovation.

Large outfits can try to influence that state of affairs by trying
to hire a superior subset of the people, by understanding better what
they are trying to do, and by inter-disciplinarity (an Interval model).
Or you can try to do, as a small entrepreneur startup, what you think
you understand, and hope to be one of the startups that gets jumped
onto later.

The small enterprise has a big disadvantage (succeed quickly or die
quickly) and one big advantage (more focused, hopefully less
distractions), but its innovation capabilities are very comparable
to that of the multinational (proportionaly to the number of active
researchers, not that many) because it starts from nearly the same
point.

(thanks for the insight from the veterans, BTW, great thread, and down
to earth for a change :-)
