Date: Fri, 3 Dec 93 16:05:55 EST
From: eli@suneast.east.sun.com (Elias Israel - SunSelect Engineering)
Subject: Start-Up VirtSem: Charity for Entrepreneurs

Finally a VirtSem that I can sink my teeth into and, what do you know, it
starts while I'm off on vacation.

If there were reading materials, can someone please forward the list to me?
I'm trying to get access to as many sources on this topic as possible.

There's only one problem with this discussion that I can think of, that
being the secrecy question. I expect that there are a number of people on
the list trying to build new enterprises who, naturally, cannot discuss
them in great detail (if at all) for fear of "giving away the store."
When one has a workable idea, sharing it with too many others is a sure
way to ruin one's chances.

Apropos the discussion of the relationship between entrepreneurs and VC
(or wannabes and VC), I had the opportunity this week to attend the MIT
Enterprise Forum, a monthly gathering at the MIT Sloan School of
Business.  At an Enterprise Forum, one or two organizations will make a
short presentation of their business plans in front of a panel of VCs
and people who put VC together with new companies. The panel then
critiques the presentation and the business plan, offering ideas for
improvement and strategies for focusing the company, obtaining funding,
etc.

The two presenters that I saw underscored some of the points being made
in this VirtSem regarding what VC is for and when you can approach
them.

The first presenter wanted to build an ethnic theater, featuring
independently-produced films, in Roxbury, a largely minority section
of Boston. More than that, he wants to reinvigorate a now dead section
of town, virtually single-handedly. It's a wonderful vision, but it's
not yet a business. It's too complicated (it involved a real estate
deal, a cafe in the daytime hours, and possibly even a multi-media
center for use by local school children), wasn't yet completely
researched (availability of independent films, potential viewership of
those films), and may have been too small a project (his best estimates
showed only a moderately successful business). He was basically
pitching his vision, and it's a great one; it's just not yet a
business. He was advised to focus on the business aspects over the
vision, try to spin off some of the complexity onto partners or
strategic alliances, and think about a bigger enterprise (he had said
that more than 20 other cities might be targets for theaters like the
one he wants to build).

The second presenter had something concrete. He and some of his MIT
friends had developed a prototype for a portable image scanner about
the size of a pen that you could use to capture fragments of text. He
had the opposite problem from the first presenter. He had something
concrete that you could sell, but he had not idea to whom to sell it,
why they would buy it, and what they would pay. In the absence of a
positioning statement to the contrary, he was basically going up
against people like Logitech, and didn't know how he was going to deal
with that. He had a product, but no vision and no company. He was
advised to find a strategic alliance, or possibly to sell the product
idea outright to a medium- or large-sized company. I think the panel
felt that he and his friends were a bunch of techies not well suited to
running a company and not a team they'd risk money on.

It came up over and over, "we don't buy ideas, we buy people." What VC
wants to see is a concrete statement of business goals, a credible plan
for achieving them, and a team that can be trusted to carry it out.

I hope this VirtSem goes on a bit longer, I'm trying to learn all that I
can about this topic.

Elias Israel
eli@east.sun.com
