Interestingly, it was at an "MIT Club"/New Enterprise Forum at Stanford
(a lot of MITers at Stanford or in the area) that my start-up friends
(doing the localizers) met their Trimble Navigation partner. I went to
one and reported on it here, about 8-9 months ago...the "tabletop
water distiller" that uses freezing to beat out boiling systems--I was
deeply impressed by them and chastened to hear about they'd been
unable to get reasonable financing. 

> The two presenters that I saw underscored some of the points being made
> in this VirtSem regarding what VC is for and when you can approach
> them.

A track record of previous start-up successes, or at least management
of successful product group within a company (which requires
management skill, understanding of P&L statements, meeting a deadline
for shipping a product, etc.), is the key. I say this, Jamie says
this, others say this.

My advice to would-be entrepreneurs *in high tech* (which is my
focus...the advice may not apply to opening a bookstore in Sturgis,
South Dakota) is this:

---> Either become extremely proficient in some niche of high-tech
thay you can then be one of the core technical people in a start-up
organized by others (perhaps commercializing your Ph.D. thesis work on
ion-beam milling, for example), or,

---> Rise through the management ranks at an established company and
learn how to do what I mentioned above: meet a real schedule, manage
people under you to do this, understand Profit and Loss calculations
for your project, etc.

Most of the chip companies I have seen here in this area--and I have
seen friends, coworkers, and research partners form Cypress, Seeq,
Atmel, Actel, Zilog, Sundisk, Waferscale, and a dozen others--were
formed with a specific product portfolio in mind, a fast track
schedule to get the products out so that the "burn rate" of seed
capital can be throttled back, and a relatively savvy bunch of
founders. Some of them were bozos of course.

The number of successful start-ups formed by people in their 20s who
have no compelling product idea and little business experience is
nearly zip. Steve Jobs and Steve Wozniak built Apple, but with some
critical differences:

- the Apple I had already been built and sold (in limited numbers)

- the Apple II was finished and could be demoed to investors...I saw
it demoed in late 1976 or early 1977, as I recall, at the Homebrew
Computer Club. (Not to pick on Xanadu, but after two decades of work
and the last 5 years of funding by Autodesk, where's a demonstratable
product?)

- Mike Markkula was leaving Intel about the time I joined, in 1974. He
had enough money and enough businesss sense (Marketing manager of
Intel, I recall) to recognize what was needed to bring the Apple II to
market. Within several months of financing, the market introduction
occurered, There was no 5-year development cycle.

So, bear this in mind. Garage shop start-ups are still possible (as
with the Salient Software "Disk Doubler" product I described a while
back), but there are few "change the world vision" garage start-ups.

> I hope this VirtSem goes on a bit longer, I'm trying to learn all that I
> can about this topic.

With good contributions like Elias', maybe it will.

